\chapter{Direct C11 and the generated runtime}
\label{ch:81}
\index{Direct C11 and the generated runtime}

The reference backend translates checked HIR into C11 and uses private runtime entry points for operations that need checked arithmetic, allocation, managed environments, resource cleanup, or runtime faults. JSON and cooperative concurrency add companion sources when needed. Shapes and names in the generated code follow compiler layout rules. They are private implementation details with different stability from SDK APIs.

\section{Read the output in execution order}
Emit C for a closure factory, locate the typed invocation function and environment constructor, then follow the call site and cleanup. Repeat with an ordinary top-level function converted to a function value to see how the environment differs. If behavior depends on a trap or cleanup after an early return, compare the generated statements and the actual executable. Inspect the helper's link plan for missing runtime functions before debugging generated code. Direct C11 can admit profiles that MIR still rejects.

\section{Count environments, not variables}
Suppose \OnxCodeTerm{add(1)} returns a closure that adds its captured
one to an integer. Bind the result to an immutable name, copy it into a
mutable function variable, and keep a third alias. There are three names
but only one live captured environment. Calling any of them with 41 must
produce 42. In the generated-code ABI a function argument borrows its
environment during the call, while a returned function value carries an
owned reference. Returning from the factory must therefore leave the
capture alive after its creating invocation has ended.

Assign the mutable variable to itself, then replace it with
\OnxCodeTerm{add(2)}. Self-assignment must leave the old value callable.
After replacement there are two live environments: the new one held by
the mutable variable and the original one reachable through the other
names. Calling the mutable variable with 41 yields 43; calling the old
alias still yields 42. A test of the mutable variable alone would miss
premature destruction of the first environment. On leaving the scope,
the last references go away and the live count should fall to zero.

Read the generated cleanup with these counts in hand. A retain is not a
fresh allocation, and a release is not necessarily a destruction: it may
only relinquish one of several references. For a capturing factory, an
allocation counter can distinguish sharing from making a distinct heap
copy for every alias. For a capture-free function value, first establish
whether an environment was allocated at all before expecting a destructor
call in the generated C.

